            <ArticleTitle>[Diagnostic value of measuring resistance of airway in the bronchus diastole test for asthma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>381-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss the variation of resistance of airway (Raw) and the diagnostic value of the bronchus diastole test for asthma patient.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The forced expiratory volume of the first second (FEV1) and Raw were measured.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">1. The positive rate of FEV1 increase after the test was 68% (20/31). 2. There was a significant difference in percentage of FEV1 increase after the test between positive group and negative group. 3. After the bronchus diastole test, the Raw decreased significantly in both groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The decrease of Raw after bronchus diastole test is more valuable for the asthma diagnosis than that of FEV1.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
